WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.890
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.440
This is not an episode about a paper, but
it's about the series itself.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.269
There are some minor changes coming, and I
am trying my very best to make it as enjoyable

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:18.949
as possible to you, so I would really like
to hear your opinion on an issue.

00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:24.180
Many of our episodes are on new topics where
I am trying my best to cover the basics so

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.040
that the scope of a new research work can
be understood clearly.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.000
However, as we are continuing our journey
deeper into the depths of state of the art

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.469
research, it inevitably happens that we have
to build on already existing knowledge from

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:39.920
earlier episodes.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.340
The big question is, how we should handle
such cases.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:48.410
For instance, in the case of a neural network
paper, the solution we went for so far was

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.040
having a quick recap for what a neural network
is.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.320
We can either have this recap in every episode
about for instance, neural networks, fluid

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.920
simulations or photorealistic rendering and
be insidiously annoying to our seasoned Fellow

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.799
Scholars who know it all.

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:09.560
Or, we don't talk about the preliminaries
to cater to the more seasoned Fellow Scholars

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.930
out there, at the expense new people who are
locked out of the conversation, as they may

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:19.100
be watching their very first episode of Two
Minute Papers.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.170
So the goal is clear, I'd like the episodes
to be as easily understandable as possible,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:29.689
but while keeping the narrative intact so
that every term I use is explained in the

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:30.689
episode.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:35.140
First, I was thinking about handing out a
so called "dictionary" in the video description

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.600
box where all of these terms would be explained
briefly.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.280
At first, this sounded like a good idea, but
most people new to the series would likely

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.030
not know about it, and for them, the fact
that these episodes are not self-contained

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.360
anymore would perhaps be confusing, or even
worse, repulsive.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:58.140
The next idea was that, perhaps, instead of
re-explaining these terms over and over again,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.659
we could add an overlay text in the video
for them.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:05.930
The more seasoned Fellow Scholars won't be
held up because they know what a Lagrangian

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:11.250
fluid simulation is, but someone new to the
series could also catch up easily just by

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.540
reading a line of text that pops up.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.320
I think this one would be a formidable solution.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.319
I would love to know your opinion on these
possible solutions, I personally think that

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:26.260
the overlay text is the best, but who knows,
maybe a better idea gets raised.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.160
Please make sure to let me know below in the
comments section whether you have started

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.200
watching Two Minute Papers recently or maybe
you're a seasoned Fellow Scholar, and how

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.180
you feel about the issue.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.480
Have you ever encountered terms that you didn't
understand?

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.120
Or was it the opposite, am I beating a dead
horse with re-explaining all this simple stuff?

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.310
I'd like to make these episodes the best I
possibly can so that seasoned Fellow Scholars

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:56.430
and people new to the show alike can marvel
at the wonders of research.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.989
All feedback is welcome and please make sure
to leave a comment so I can better understand

00:03:00.989 --> 00:03:04.069
how you feel about this issue and what would
make you happier.

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:08.629
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

